The World Is Resisting Trump On FTA. So Should India
Unable to compete with developed countries specially China, facing mounting adverse balance of trade of ~ $1.2 trillion, rising internal debt of over $38 trillion, an annual interest payment to service borrowing of ~$1 trillion, a steep fall in manufacturing, increasing challenge from China in the realm of research and innovation in emerging technologies, the American Congress and Executive under Trump is seeking to impose so called FTAs on its competitors by threatening them with up to 100% tariffs, sanctions and penalties for evasion of sanctions under the newly enacted Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026. Trump is attempting to leverage the size of the US economy to impose unfair trade agreements.

The bad news for Trump is that its not really working. The total GDP of countries (mainly Canada, Mexico, South Korea and Australia) that have agreed to full FTAs with US is only about $11 trillion in nominal GDP. The GDP of countries that have not signed full FTA is over $70 trillion in nominal terms and about $130 trillion in PPP terms.  The top ten countries both in terms of nominal GDP and GDP in PPP terms have not agreed to sign the FTAs. These countries are: China Germany Japan United Kingdom India   France  Russia Italy Brazil and Indonesia indeed EU as a whole has not signed comprehensive FTA with the US.

Promoting US As The Hub Of Global Economy
Trump sent Marco Rubio to tighten the screws on India essentially by threatening to impose both sanctions on Indian entities  as well as up to 100% tariffs on Indian imports into the US for importing Russian crude unless India agrees to ink the India-US FTA. There is no doubt that Trump looks at India as perhaps the weakest among the top countries resisting comprehensive FTA with the US. The strange feature of the FTA offer from Trump is that it is one on one with countries, the negotiating text being kept secret, and countries being made to somehow believe that they would have an edge over other countries in relative terms of the FTAs. The model is one central hub, the US, and all countries on the periphery like spokes connected to the central hub  vying for a deal with the US. The figure below shows GDPs in PPP terms. The model of a Chinese hub competing with the US hub is actually more compelling. It's shown in the next message and comment.

The Whole Argument For  Venezuelan Oil & Imposing Penal Tariffs Is Childish
Rubio told (I.E. 21st Oct 2026) the Indian Express’s Shubhajit Roy: “President issued those waivers and did not impose those tariffs required under law passed by our Congress”. He is saying that  Trump has already done a great favour to India, now it is India’s turn to reciprocate by signing the Trade Deal, and also by importing stolen very heavy Venezuelan crude to be processed at Jamnagar. But we are not asking, because of our colonial submissive mindset: “What right has the US Congress to dictate to India from where and from whom we should buy our crude oil?”
Next he thunders: “The whole world is diversifying how we get our energy. I don’t think we want a world in which an overwhelming amount of global energy comes from one part (West Asia) of the world that is prone to conflict”. Why not?  The war was imposed on Iran and the rest of Middle East by insane bombing of Iran by US and Israel. 
Then he says: “We have Venezuela that is going to be rapidly increasing the production of oil and crude very heavy crude which can only be refined in certain parts of the world. One of those places is the Gulf Coast of the United States. But one of the few other places in the world that can actually refine Venezuelan heavy crude is India and Reliance”. But Iranian and Saudi crude are more suited to the refinery of Jamnagar than the very heavy Venezuelan crude.

But  the main point is that there is actually very little existing capacity in Venezuela to meet India’s oil needs and that even if all the Venezuelan crude production was diverted to Jamnagar (1.1 million barrels per day) it would meet only about 20% of India’s oil requirement. There are a string of refineries along the coast of Texas that survive on import of Venezuelan crude, almost 60%. So what is the point of putting a gun on Modi Govts head and threatening up to 100% tariffs and sanctions if oil imports from Russia are not addressed? India requires 5.2 million barrels of crude per day. 

Earlier Ambassador Gor rather stupidly threatened: “I do think the (US) President will make a determination on that (on imposing sanctions)  over the next few days… We have ongoing discussions on that as it passed with overwhelming bipartisan support. That law got 86 votes out of 100 in our Senate”. As if India is a colony of US and we are accountable to US Congress!

Venezuelan Crude is Relatively Bad
The criteria for evaluating desirability of crude for conversion to petrol and diesel depends on the following:
API gravity (density): Higher API = lighter oil with more naturally occurring naphtha, kerosene, and gasoil fractions that convert relatively easily into petrol and diesel. Lower API = heavier oil that yields far more residual bottoms after distillation.
Sulfur, metals, and asphaltenes: Higher levels require more intensive hydrotreating, dementalization, and conversion.
Residue conversion needs: Heavy crudes leave large volumes of low-value residue that must be cracked or coked into lighter products.
Light Arabian / Iranian Crude.
Arab Light ~32–34° API, Iran Light ~33° API (medium-light to light range).
These give higher straight-run yields of middle distillates and naphtha. Less secondary conversion is needed; they are relatively straightforward for a modern refinery
Very heavy Venezuelan grades.
Merey 16 ~15–16° API, Boscan or Orinoco extra-heavy ~8–12° API.
Dense, viscous, high-sulfur, high in metals (nickel/vanadium), high in asphaltenes. Distillation produces a large volume of vacuum residue that must be upgraded (via delayed coking, hydrocracking, resid FCC, etc.) to maximize petrol and diesel. This is more expensive, uses more energy, hydrogen, and specialized units.
Shipping Time
Saudi & Iranian.  2-3 days
Venezuelan  30-45 days via Cape of Good Hope
25 days Via Suez Canal.

Trump Is Orchestrating Tensions in ME to Sell Venezuelan & US Crude
Average crude prices in 2025 as per (US) EIA data was :
Brent: ~$69.14/bbl annual average
West Texas Intermediate: ~$65.39/bbl.
Dubai/Oman (Middle East benchmark): ~$69.4/bbl.
The only reason that Venezuelan crude destined for Jamnagar can compete with West Asian crude is because  insurance premium is very low less than 10 cents per barrel compared to 7-8 $ per barrel of crude coming through Straits of Hormuz. Otherwise the transportation (and extraction costs) cost is far far higher for Venezuelan and US crude destined for India  than the transportation cost for West Asian crude going to India and China. So it makes sense for Trump to keep West Asia on the boil. 

Trump Will Attack Iran Again 
But West Asian crude is finding ways to beat the Iran-US deadlock over Hormuz and Houthi threat over Bab El Mandeb. Huge quantities of Middle East oil, mainly UAE but also Saudi Kuwaiti and Qatari oil is flowing through UAE's Fujairah port bypassing Hormuz. Despite Houthi threats oil  flows have jumped through Bab El Mandeb. Iraqi oil is flowing from Kirkuk to Ceyhan on the Mediterranean Sea through pipeline.  Ship to Ship transfers of Saudi Qatari and Kuwaiti oil is taking place at a feverish pace in the Gulf of Oman in and around Sohar port. Iranian oil is being transported through land routes and some of it is being commingled in Iraqi and Qatari crude. Recent data shows greater flow of oil through Hormuz.  
According to Kpler ship-tracking data cited across recent reports (including the Wall Street Journal on October 21, 2026), combined Middle East oil flows—Hormuz transits plus Gulf of Oman ship-to-ship (STS) transfers, Fujairah/Omani terminal loadings, and Red Sea exports—averaged more than 24 million barrels per day (bpd) in the week through October 18. That exceeds the pre-war level of roughly 23.3–24.4 million bpd. Hormuz-specific flows (including offshore transfers in the Gulf of Oman) were around 14 million bpd, or about 82% of pre-war levels!
So Trump must strike Iran again, to keep insurance rates high  and keep investing in oil extraction infrastructure in Venezuela. US bombing of Iran should resume to address this calamitous flow of oil from West Asia.

We Are Dealing With Thugs
In a detailed article I wrote on 15th June on  X, I explained the systematic, open and shameless thuggery by US under Trump. He is ramping up production of Venezuelan crude and will pressurise Modi Govt and Reliance to process it at Jamnagar. Not only must Modi continue to resist this thuggery, because it is based on orchestrating tensions in the Middle to keep oil prices high. The Opposition too must strengthen Modi's hands. The Govt must keep open channels of communication with FTA holdouts, especially China, Russia, Brazil and EU (notwithstanding the Turnberry Agreement) for a coordinated response to US FTA advances. The pictures of Hardeep Puri talking to Epstein that have emerged while Marco Rubio was in Jamnagar are attempts to intimidate and hustle India into signing the FTA. Regardless of the demerits of Puri's dealings with Epstein in since 2014 on behalf of International Peace Institute and Terje Rod-Larsen (who has publicly expressed regret over dealing with Epstein), the Modi Govt must NOT increase the already substantial crude import (~9% of our total oil requirement) from Venezuela. In fact to show the steel in our spine and our national interest to keep price of oil low, imports from Venezuela should face a token reduction. Modi Govt must not reduce crude imports from Russia, our most trusted ally. It must guard against allowing use of Indian ports for logistic support to  US Navy in the now expected US  attack on Iran, not only because Iran has been  a friendly country, but also because logistic support to US Navy in a war situation would be a hostile act against Iran. Iranian missiles flying over Pakistani (not Jordan) airspace can strike Jamnagar which is only 1000 kms from Iranian border, which is much closer than the distance between Tehran and Tel Aviv.
