Red Friday is a grassroots tradition observed primarily in the United States to honor and support American military service members who are deployed overseas.

The core practice is simple: people wear red clothing (often a red shirt) every Friday. The color is used as a visual reminder and acronym for “Remember Everyone Deployed.” Participants—military families, veterans, friends, and civilians—wear red as a quiet show of solidarity so that those serving far from home, and the families waiting for them, know they are not forgotten.

The custom is not an official military program. It grew organically within military communities, especially among Marine Corps, Army, Navy, Air Force, and National Guard families. It gained wider visibility through social media, military spouse networks, and local “Red Shirt Friday” groups. Some versions of the message also include a short poem or statement that begins with the line “I wear red on Fridays to remember everyone deployed…” and ends with a promise to keep wearing red until all service members return home.

Beyond the clothing, the tradition serves several practical and emotional purposes:
It keeps deployed personnel visible in everyday life rather than out of sight and out of mind.
It offers a low-key way for communities to show support without requiring formal events or fundraisers.
It creates a shared signal that military families can recognize in workplaces, schools, and public spaces.

While most strongly associated with U.S. forces, similar “wear red” gestures have occasionally appeared in other countries with deployed troops. The movement remains informal, voluntary, and focused on remembrance rather than politics or protest.

In short, Red Friday is a recurring, visible act of memory: one day each week when red clothing becomes a collective reminder that service members are still deployed and that people at home are thinking of them.

